'''Habax''' (from {{Langx|ar|حبش|Ḥabash|mixture}}) is the capital of [[Cabu Verd]], an insular autonomous [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of [[Araucaria]], within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. It is situated on the island of [[Favaritx]]. The harbour of Alhambra has long been a leading tourist destination in Balisca, receiving more than a million tourists through its main port in 2017, and was historically a prominent destination for commercial and {{W|Ocean liners|trans-Aethiopic passenger cruises}} between [[Kafto-Illypnia]] and [[Abyala]]. It is one of the most famous monuments to {{W|Mudéjar art|Mudéjar architecture}} and one of the best-preserved displays of the historic Kafto-Mozbayan culture in the world.

The foundation stone of the city was laid by [[Mozbayani]]-speaking Muslim traders from [[Andalus]] on 28 March 1526, for what later became the [[Great Mosque of Habax|Gran Mezquita de Habax]]. Following the consolidation of Andalusi territorial control of the archipelago, Habax was selected as seat of the newly created [[Colony of Abyssinia]]. The city is one of the [[List of oldest continuously inhabited cities#Abyala|oldest continually inhabited cities in Abyala]].

Like most cities within Araucaria, Habax has a long, multicultural past, and this has been reflected in the city's distinct culture: it has developed its own music, famous cuisine (to include the staple {{W|couscous|Cuscús}}), unique dialects, and annual celebrations and festivals, most notably [[W:Mawlid|Mawlid]] (and related ''[[W:Mehfil|mehfil]]''). 

